WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.200
Marvel Studios may have been giddy beyond
compare about re-releasing Avengers: Endgame,

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.160
but some Marvel fans feel differently.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:12.290
The house of heroes launched a special version
of Avengers: Endgame in theaters on June 28th,

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:16.590
offering viewers a chance to see this year's
biggest superhero movie with a few extra features

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.380
tacked on to either end of its three-hour
runtime.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.920
Not an extended or alternate cut of the film,
the Endgame re-release included an introduction

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.890
from co-director Anthony Russo, a tribute
to the late Stan Lee, an unfinished deleted

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:32.950
scene focused on Mark Ruffalo's Professor
Hulk, and footage from Spider-Man: Far From

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.660
Home, the final film in Phase 3 of the Marvel
Cinematic Universe.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.770
Many Marvelites headed out to theaters over
the weekend to feast their eyes on the Avengers:

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:44.230
Endgame re-release and were solely disappointed
by what they saw.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:48.390
From misunderstanding what kind of footage
the re-release would feature to being underwhelmed

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:52.860
by the post-credits extras to even finding
the bonus content cheap-looking, fans are

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.100
upset by the Endgame re-release.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.390
And they are tweeting that rage.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.380
@nctaliaromanova kicked things off, saying,

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.870
"Save your money for black widow (2020) instead
of the endgame re-release because i've never

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.759
been so disappointed in my life."

00:01:05.759 --> 00:01:07.630
Twitter user @natasharogers_ wrote

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:12.149
"I am so disappointed and strongly recommend
you not watching it, it is a waste of money."

00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:16.640
Meanwhile, @bizgamer9 said he liked it fine,
but that's because he just wanted to see the

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:17.640
movie
again.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.200
"If that hadn't been the case I would be more
disappointed with what they added.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.969
Seriously, if you're going in wanting new
stuff then don't go at all and wait for the

00:01:24.969 --> 00:01:25.969
Blu-ray."

00:01:25.969 --> 00:01:29.710
Countless others were upset with themselves
for not doing enough fact-finding about the

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.219
Endgame re-release beforehand.

00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:34.579
One fan said they were, quote, "literally
a fool" for thinking that they would get to

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:39.030
see the deleted scene featuring Katherine
Langford as an older Morgan Stark, the daughter

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.060
of Robert Downey Jr.'s Tony Stark.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.060
“Hahaha!

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.060
You fool!

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.840
You fell victim to one of the classic blunders!”

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.440
@daniel6711_ summed things up, writing,

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.200
"The deleted scenes they added at the end
of the movie were the most pointless scenes.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.579
It was a hulk deleted scene and a far from
home sneak peek.

00:01:54.579 --> 00:01:57.959
The only thing nice about it was the Stan
Lee tribute and the free poster."

00:01:57.959 --> 00:02:02.280
Many Marvel fans are particularly angry about
the poor quality of the never-before-seen

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.280
Hulk scene.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:04.280
@FilmCriticKurt tweeted,

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.140
"I'm so disappointed and furious with the
re-release of Avengers: Endgame.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.590
The new footage was terrible.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:13.890
PS2 level CGI with no impact on story and
some fluff content.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.780
Do not spend money on this!"

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.030
Added user @ZachCR_,

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:22.170
"Tbh the re-release bonus content for Avengers
EndGame was really poor quality, and almost

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.360
looked Fan-Made...

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.870
Seems like kind of a cash grab.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.030
Very disappointed."

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:30.480
The idea that the re-release was spurred by
a desire to make more money is, of course,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.090
obviously true.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:35.470
Marvel was specifically hoping that revenue
from the re-release would allow the film to

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:40.470
pass James Cameron's Avatar as the highest
grossing film in box office history, as Endgame

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.760
entered the weekend trailing by just $37 million.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.070
It seems that some fans were outraged by the
lengths Marvel and parent company Disney went

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:52.260
to squeeze more juice out of Endgame, with
@SherrieGies slamming the re-release as a

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:56.250
"[...] blatant cash grab by disney bc they
know they'll make millions off of ppl who

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.170
live and breathe for marvel movies [...]"

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:02.730
To make matters worse, Avengers: Endgame didn't
even end up beating Avatar with its re-release

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.660
over the weekend, taking in just $5.5 million
domestically.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:11.160
It looks like it may never surpass the Cameron-directed
film, with The Hollywood Reporter writing

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.540
that media analysts believe that, quote, "it
would be impossible" for Avengers: Endgame

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.880
to top Avatar unless Marvel re-released the
film again at some point in the future.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.070
Everything considered, it seems like the bonus
content included in the Avengers: Endgame

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:27.540
re-release wasn't worth the cost of entry
for many Marvel fans.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.120
It's unlikely that this backlash will tarnish
the studio's reputation, given just how beloved

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.710
the MCU is, but it will probably make Marvel
and Disney think twice about how they approach

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:39.850
re-releasing movies down the line… if they
ever do.

